  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework's performance and obstacle detection accuracy were validated on a relatively small sample of three local sites.",
      "what": "This study develops a neighborhood-scale barrier-free accessibility assessment framework using handheld LiDAR point clouds. The method detects ground obstacles at a 0.5 m grid resolution using elevation interquartile range values to build an obstacle cost map, then applies an eight-neighbor reverse Dijkstra algorithm to calculate accessible areas and travel distances. Tested across three sites (a flat neighborhood, a hilly neighborhood, and a school), the obstacle detection achieved a Precision of 88.2% and an F1-score of 85.8%, while simulated barrier-free modifications increased the accessible area in the hilly site from 14,501 to 68,761 square meters.",
      "why": "It provides a highly detailed, quantitative method to diagnose micro-scale physical barriers and simulate the impact of specific infrastructure modifications on wheelchair routing before they are built."
    }
  },
